# GuiOS desktop chrome: the sidebar has its declared width, the taskbar is a
# strip at the bottom, and both are delineated. This is the frame the GuiOS
# chrome work was about, and until 2026-07-21 it could not be recorded at all.
#
# Two things had to be true before a golden was possible.
#
# The clock. GuiShell paints the RTC date and time into the taskbar and the
# dashboard every frame, so two captures were never byte-identical and the
# honest conclusion was "guios cannot have a golden". That was a statement
# about the machine, not the app: codex-vm answered the CMOS RTC from the
# host clock and offered no way to say otherwise. `vmargs -rtc` is that way.
# The stamp is arbitrary; it only has to be fixed.
#
# The view. F3 is chosen deliberately over the F1 dashboard, which is the more
# attractive frame and the wrong subject: it prints `Uptime: <ticks/18>s` and a
# heap figure, and at an 11-second deadline the uptime divides within one tick
# of a boundary, so it flips between 10 and 11 under host load. Freezing the
# RTC would NOT fix that, and a tolerance would only hide it. Uptime is
# legitimately not a function of the program, so it is kept out of the frame
# rather than lied about. The hardware view is static text, which leaves the
# chrome and the frozen clock as the whole of the image.
#
# What this can therefore catch: the sidebar taking half the desktop (it did),
# the taskbar taking half the right-hand column (it did), a panel with no
# visible extent (every one of them had none), and any future regression in
# widget layout, theme resolution, border emission or the depth rule.

vmargs -rtc 2026-07-21T09:41:07

# The wait is not padding. GuiOS runs at about 2.2 frames a second, so the F3
# key has to be polled and then a whole frame has to be composited before the
# view has changed on the glass. At a 11750 ms deadline that raced: a -Accept
# run recorded the F1 dashboard instead, and the taskbar showing [Sys] rather
# than [HW] is the only thing that gives it away. Recording the wrong frame is
# the failure mode -Accept has, and looking at the picture is the only guard.
boot 9000
key 61
wait 2000
settle 3000
